---
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import { supabaseUrl } from "../../lib/supabase-public";

const base = `${supabaseUrl}/storage/v1/object/public/materuni-images/`;
const coffeeHero = base + "coffee_image/1791998412318-materuni-tour.jpg";
const pickingImage = base + "coffee_image/1791994933291-materuni-coffe-picking-lesson.jpg";

const blogPostSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BlogPosting",
  "headline": "How Traditional Chagga Coffee is Made from Bean to Cup",
  "description": "Step-by-step guide to traditional Chagga Arabica coffee production in Materuni Village: hand-picking, wooden pestle hulling, open woodfire roasting, and ancestral singing.",
  "image": "https://materunihosts.com/social-preview.jpg",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-20T08:00:00+03:00",
  "dateModified": "2026-10-20T08:00:00+03:00",
  "author": [
    {
      "@type": "Person",
      "name": "Elia",
      "jobTitle": "Chagga Coffee Specialist"
    },
    {
      "@type": "Person",
      "name": "Saidi Materu",
      "jobTitle": "Founder & Host"
    }
  ],
  "publisher": {
    "@type": "TourOperator",
    "name": "Materuni Hosts",
    "logo": {
      "@type": "ImageObject",
      "url": "https://materunihosts.com/materuni-hosts-logo-round.svg"
    }
  },
  "mainEntityOfPage": {
    "@type": "WebPage",
    "@id": "https://materunihosts.com/journal/how-traditional-chagga-coffee-is-made"
  }
};

const faqList = [
  {
    q: "What type of coffee is grown in Materuni?",
    a: "Materuni sits between 1,600 and 1,800 metres above sea level on volcanic soil, making it ideal for organic Arabica coffee (predominantly Bourbon and Kent varieties). The high altitude and shade from banana trees yield dense, aromatic beans with bright citrus and cocoa notes."
  },
  {
    q: "Why do Chagga hosts sing while grinding coffee?",
    a: "Chagga coffee songs establish a communal work rhythm for the heavy wooden pestle and mortar ('kinu na mti'). The energetic beat keeps participants in sync, turns hard labour into a festive celebration, and connects generations through ancestral storytelling."
  },
  {
    q: "Can I buy freshly roasted coffee beans to take home?",
    a: "Yes. Guests on our tours can purchase whole bean or medium-ground 250g and 500g bags packaged directly by local village farming families. 100% of the proceeds stay with the grower households."
  }
];

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": faqList.map((f) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    "name": f.q,
    "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": f.a
    }
  }))
};
---

<SiteLayout
  title="How Traditional Chagga Coffee is Made from Bean to Cup | Materuni Journal"
  description="Discover the authentic steps of organic Chagga Arabica coffee production in Materuni Village: harvesting, peeling, woodfire roasting, and pestle grinding."
  canonical="https://materunihosts.com/journal/how-traditional-chagga-coffee-is-made"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(blogPostSchema)} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <article class="article-wrap">
    <header class="article-header">
      <nav class="article-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumbs">
        <a href="/">Home</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
        <a href="/journal">Journal</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
        <span>Culture &amp; food</span>
      </nav>

      <span class="eyebrow">CULTURE &amp; FOOD · MATERUNI TRADITIONS</span>
      <h1>How Traditional Chagga Coffee is Made from Bean to Cup</h1>

      <div class="article-meta">
        <span class="meta-authors">By <strong>Elia</strong> &amp; <strong>Saidi Materu</strong></span>
        <span class="meta-dot">·</span>
        <time datetime="2026-10-20">October 20, 2026</time>
        <span class="meta-dot">·</span>
        <span class="meta-reading-time">4 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <figure class="article-featured-media">
      <Image
        src={coffeeHero}
        alt="Roasting organic coffee beans over an open campfire in Materuni Village"
        width={1200}
        height={675}
        format="webp"
        quality={82}
        loading="eager"
        decoding="async"
        class="featured-img"
      />
      <figcaption>Freshly ground Arabica coffee brewed in a traditional pot over fragrant eucalyptus wood.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="article-body">
      <p>On the fertile volcanic slopes of Mount Kilimanjaro, coffee is much more than an agricultural commodity—it is the beating heart of Chagga family life. In Materuni Village, high at 1,700 metres elevation, every family farm (known locally as a <em>shamba</em>) intercrops organic Arabica coffee trees underneath tall, protective banana canopies.</p>

      <p>When you join our <a href="/materuni-coffee-tour">Materuni Coffee Tour</a> or the full <a href="/experiences">Waterfall &amp; Coffee Experience</a>, you participate in every single step of this century-old ritual. Here is how that journey looks.</p>

      <hr class="divider" />

      <h2>Step 1: Harvesting the Red 'Kahawa' Cherries</h2>
      <p>Coffee harvest in Materuni peaks between July and December. Unlike industrial plantations that strip whole branches, Chagga families hand-pick only the deep ruby-red cherries that have ripened to peak sweetness under the mountain sun.</p>

      <figure class="in-content-media">
        <Image
          src={pickingImage}
          alt="Hands inspecting ripe red coffee cherries on an Arabica branch"
          width={900}
          height={500}
          format="webp"
          quality={80}
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>Hand-selecting ripe Arabica cherries on a village family farm in Materuni.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Step 2: Peeling and Sun-Drying</h2>
      <p>Once picked, the cherries are pulped using a manual wooden or metal depulper to strip away the sweet fruit pulp and expose the twin beans inside. The beans are washed in natural mountain spring water and dried in the sun on raised woven drying beds until the moisture content drops to exactly 11–12%.</p>

      <h2>Step 3: Removing the Parchment (Hulling)</h2>
      <p>Inside the dried bean is a papery protective husk called parchment (<em>pergamino</em>). We place the sun-dried beans into a large carved wooden mortar (<em>kinu</em>) and pound them rhythmically using a heavy wooden pestle (<em>mti</em>). Winnowing with a woven basket blows away the dry husk, leaving pristine green coffee beans.</p>

      <h2>Step 4: Woodfire Roasting in Clay or Iron Pots</h2>
      <p>The green beans are poured into a wide iron pan placed over hot embers of indigenous firewood. As the heat rises, the beans turn from pale straw to gold, then to cinnamon brown. Soon, the famous "first crack" sounds like popping corn. Smoke scented with brown sugar, dark cocoa, and toasted nuts fills the air as your host expertly stirs to prevent scorching.</p>

      <h2>Step 5: Pestle Grinding to the Beat of Chagga Songs</h2>
      <p>While still hot, the roasted beans return to the giant wooden kinu. This is where the magic happens: your hosts lead high-spirited Chagga songs, synchronizing the downward strike of the pestles to the musical tempo. Everyone takes turns grinding to the beat, singing chorus calls that echo across the valley.</p>

      <h2>Step 6: Boiling and Pouring</h2>
      <p>The finely crushed aromatic powder is added directly into bubbling mountain water in a kettle over the fire. After a gentle steep, the steaming brew is strained into cups. The result is sweet, smooth, and full-bodied—without any trace of harsh bitterness.</p>

      <hr class="divider" />

      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-accordion">
        {faqList.map((item) => (
          <details class="faq-card">
            <summary>
              <span>{item.q}</span>
              <span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-body">
              <p>{item.a}</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="booking-cta-card">
        <div class="cta-inner">
          <span class="eyebrow">TASTE IT YOURSELF</span>
          <h3>Brew your own cup with a Chagga family.</h3>
          <p>Join our 4–5 hour hands-on coffee roasting workshop or combine it with the 95m waterfall hike for a full day of adventure.</p>
          <div class="cta-actions">
            <a class="gold-button" href="/booking?experience=coffee">Book coffee experience ($45)</a>
            <a class="outline-button" href="https://wa.me/255612068540?text=Hello%20Materuni%20Hosts%2C%20I%20would%20love%20to%20join%20a%20coffee%20tour." target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ask on WhatsApp</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>

